---
/**
 * website/src/pages/mobile.astro — the phone app's page (the zoom-diagrams plan,
 * `docs/website/zoom-diagrams/implementation-plan.md` §3, **Part 3**; the owner's answers of
 * 2026-10-21 under D239, D240, D241 and D242).
 *
 * **The page is a preview, and it says so three times over** (D240's three conditions). The
 * badge above the headline says the app is coming; no date appears anywhere on the page — not
 * in its prose, not in its `<title>`, not in its description — because the phone app's
 * schedule is the owner's and a date here is a claim this repository cannot back; and every
 * bullet the page prints is marked **shipped** or **planned**, so no line of it is a false
 * present tense. `website/test/mobile.mjs` is what holds all three: it refuses a four-digit
 * year or a month name in the page's text, and it holds every claim to the state the data
 * file gives it.
 *
 * **Where its facts come from, and why the claims are not typed here:**
 *
 *   - `website/data/mobile-claims.yaml` — one row per claim, each with the claim's `text` and
 *     its `state`, read and validated by `src/content.ts`'s `loadMobileClaims()`. The split is
 *     the owner's, bullet by bullet (D241): status and spend are **shipped**, because the job
 *     list reads both out of the Realtime Database today; the roster, switching by name, the
 *     wake word, QR pairing and the three roles are **planned**. This page renders the rows it
 *     is handed and writes no claim of its own — a page that restated one is the copy that
 *     drifts first (Global Constraint 6).
 *   - `website/data/mobile-screens.yaml` — the phone app's screenshots, read through
 *     `loadMobileScreens()`. **The file carries no row, and that is the state this page is
 *     written to render** (the plan's Part 3, item 4): the slider below renders the rows it is
 *     handed, and a file with no row in it renders no slider, no heading and no frame at all —
 *     never a picture-shaped hole a reader could not tell from a picture that failed to arrive.
 *     The pictures are the owner's, supplied as files (D251); no agent commits a screenshot
 *     (D190).
 *
 * **The voice bullet is on-device, and the page names no speech service** (D242): the row says
 * where the listening and the recognition happen and nothing about where a transcript would be
 * sent, because the local/remote evaluation switch is design only and not built
 * (`docs/mobile/design/voice-evaluation-switch.md`, the reconciliation's ZF28). The sentence
 * lives in the data file with the other claims, so this page holds no second copy of it.
 *
 * **There is no waitlist, and nothing here asks a reader for anything** (D239, answered
 * 2026-10-21: "no waitlist"): no form, no `mailto:` and no third-party collector. The page
 * carries the badge and the copy and nothing else, which is why the privacy page, the CSP's
 * `form-action 'self'`, `linkcheck.mjs`'s `mailto:` refusal and Check 69b's "nothing phones
 * home" are all untouched by this part.
 *
 * **The page is the console page's sister, and the shape is its own.** The hero is the
 * console's (a pill above the headline, the headline, the lead), the slider is the same
 * component, and the claims are the console's facts grid with the state marked on each card —
 * a card's own label and the group's heading say which of the two a reader is looking at, so
 * the marking is visible without reading every line (the owner's visual check). Every class
 * below is this page's own and every colour is a token, so the contrast the palette check
 * computes is the contrast a reader gets (W1.2).
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import ScreenshotSlider from '../components/ScreenshotSlider.astro'
import { loadMobileClaims, loadMobileScreens } from '../content.ts'

/**
 * The lead: the page's own two sentences, and the only prose on it that is not a claim out of
 * the data file. It says what the app is for and how to read the rest of the page, and it
 * promises nothing and dates nothing — what the app does is the marked claims below it. It is
 * also the page's `<meta name="description">`, so a search result carries the same words a
 * reader meets at the top of the page rather than a second, unread sentence.
 */
const LEAD =
  "Your runs' status and spend, in your pocket. The app is in progress: every line below says whether it is here today or still to come."

// The claims, and the two lists the page renders them in. The rows come in as the data file
// writes them; the split into the two states is this page's only reading of them.
const { claims } = loadMobileClaims()

// The screenshots. There is no row in the file today, so this renders no slider at all — and
// the day the owner's files land, the section below appears with them and nothing here moves.
const { screens } = loadMobileScreens()

/** The word each state is marked with, which is the whole of what a card says about its state. */
const STATE_LABEL: Record<string, string> = { shipped: 'Shipped', planned: 'Planned' }

const shipped = claims.filter((claim) => claim.state === 'shipped')
const planned = claims.filter((claim) => claim.state === 'planned')

/**
 * Each state is rendered as its own list under its own heading, so **a list with no row in it
 * is a defect in this page rather than a section to draw empty**: a page whose planned list is
 * empty says the app is finished, and a page whose shipped list is empty says it does nothing
 * today. Both are claims about the product that this page is not allowed to make — the file's
 * reader refuses an empty file for the same reason — so the build stops here, naming the file
 * to fix, rather than shipping a heading over nothing.
 */
for (const [state, rows] of [
  ['shipped', shipped],
  ['planned', planned],
] as const) {
  if (rows.length === 0) {
    throw new Error(
      `website/src/pages/mobile.astro: ${JSON.stringify(state)} has no row in website/data/mobile-claims.yaml, and this page renders the two states as two lists under two headings: an empty list is a heading over nothing, and a page whose *${state}* list is empty claims the app has ${state === 'shipped' ? 'nothing to show today' : 'nothing left to build'} — neither of which is this page's to say (D240, D241). Add the row the state is missing, or take the page down.`,
    )
  }
}
---

<Base title="Mobile" description={LEAD}>
  <article>
    {/* The page's first screen, and the badge D240 asks for: a preview label above the
        headline, in the block that holds the headline and the lead — the shape the console
        page's own pill is drawn in, and the place a reader looks for what a page is before
        reading what it says. The mark beside it is the accent rather than the success tone the
        console's pill carries: this page is not a running thing, and a green "it is up" dot
        beside "Coming soon" would be the page contradicting itself. */}
    <section class="mobile-hero" aria-labelledby="mobile-title">
      <div class="hero-glow" aria-hidden="true"></div>

      <div class="mobile-hero-copy">
        <p class="pill mobile-badge" data-preview-badge>
          <span class="mobile-badge-dot" aria-hidden="true" />
          Coming soon
        </p>

        <h1 class="mobile-title display-xl" id="mobile-title">
          Fugaro on your <span class="gradient-text">phone</span>
        </h1>

        {/* The lead: what the app is for, and the one sentence that tells a reader how to read
            the rest of the page. It promises nothing and dates nothing — what the app does is
            the claims below, each marked with the state the data file gives it. */}
        <p class="mobile-lead">{LEAD}</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    {/* The screenshots, in the slot the console page's own slider fills. `mobile-screens.yaml`
        carries no row, so this renders nothing at all today; when the owner's files arrive,
        the section appears with its own heading, its own ids and its own name — the mobile
        page's, not the console's — and neither this file nor the component moves. */}
    <ScreenshotSlider
      screens={screens}
      slot="mobile-screens"
      idPrefix="mobile-screen"
      label="Screenshots of the phone app. Scroll sideways to see the rest."
    />

    {/* The claims, in the two states D240's second condition requires. Each state is its own
        `<section>` with its own heading, which is the console page's own shape — a section
        named by the heading over it — and the reason there are two of them here: one section
        holding both would be a region named "What the app does today" with "What is still to
        come" inside it, which is a name that describes half of what it holds. Each card is
        marked with its own state as well as standing in its own list, so which of the two a
        bullet is can be read off the heading or off the card. The `data-claims` hooks are the
        lists' names for the test that holds every row to the state the data file gives it. */}
    <section id="mobile-claims" aria-labelledby="mobile-claims-heading">
      <h2 id="mobile-claims-heading" class="display-lg">What the app does today</h2>
      <ul class="mobile-claims" data-claims="shipped">
        {
          shipped.map((claim) => (
            <li class="card mobile-claim" data-state={claim.state}>
              <p class="mobile-claim-state">{STATE_LABEL[claim.state]}</p>
              <p class="mobile-claim-text">{claim.text}</p>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="mobile-planned" aria-labelledby="mobile-planned-heading">
      <h2 id="mobile-planned-heading" class="display-lg">What is still to come</h2>
      <ul class="mobile-claims" data-claims="planned">
        {
          planned.map((claim) => (
            <li class="card mobile-claim" data-state={claim.state}>
              <p class="mobile-claim-state">{STATE_LABEL[claim.state]}</p>
              <p class="mobile-claim-text">{claim.text}</p>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  /* Tokens only (W1.2): every colour below is the palette in force, so the contrast check in
     `website/test/a11y.mjs` is about the colours this page actually paints. */

  /* The hero: the console page's centred first screen, with the site's own glow behind it
     (`base.css`'s `.hero-glow`, which needs a positioned, clipped parent). */
  .mobile-hero {
    position: relative;
    overflow: hidden;
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-2xl);
  }

  .mobile-hero-copy {
    position: relative;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-md);
    padding: var(--space-2xl) 0 var(--space-xl);
    text-align: center;
  }

  .mobile-badge {
    gap: var(--space-2xs);
    font-size: var(--font-size-sm);
  }

  /* The badge's dot: the accent, which is the colour the site marks a thing that is this
     product's own. It is a mark and not text, so it carries no contrast pair of its own. */
  .mobile-badge-dot {
    flex: none;
    width: 8px;
    height: 8px;
    border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
    background: var(--accent);
  }

  /* The headline: `base.css`'s `.display-xl`, held to the two lines the console page's own
     headline is held to rather than running the width of a wide window. */
  .mobile-title {
    max-width: 20ch;
  }

  /* The lead under it, at the size the console page sets its own lead and no wider than a
     comfortable measure. */
  .mobile-lead {
    max-width: 60ch;
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: var(--font-size-xl);
    line-height: var(--leading-snug);
  }

  /* The claims: the console page's facts grid, one list per state, so a row added to either
     state in the data file needs no rule of its own. */
  .mobile-claims {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(18rem, 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-md);
    margin: var(--space-lg) 0 var(--space-2xl);
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .mobile-claim {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-2xs);
  }

  /* The state, on the card: the small uppercase label the site's eyebrow already is, in the
     two tones the states mean — the muted text for what is planned, and the text tone for what
     has shipped, which is the one of the two a reader can act on today. The card's own edge
     says the same thing a second way, so the marking survives a reader who is skimming rather
     than reading: a shipped card carries the success tone's border, and a planned one the
     ordinary border it is drawn with. */
  .mobile-claim-state {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: var(--font-size-sm);
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
  }

  .mobile-claim[data-state='shipped'] {
    border-color: var(--ok);
  }

  .mobile-claim[data-state='shipped'] .mobile-claim-state {
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .mobile-claim-text {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }
</style>
